    "result_text": "The change is complete and verified. Here's a summary of what I did.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec requires all rejection responses (400, 409, 429) to return an RFC7807 `application/problem+json` body with `type`, `title`, `status`, and `detail`. I found three gaps on the `POST /api/owners` endpoint and fixed them the idiomatic Spring way.\n\n**1. `ExceptionControllerAdvice` \u2014 new handler for controller-raised rejections**\nAdded an `@ExceptionHandler(ResponseStatusException.class)` that reuses the existing `detailBuild(...)` helper to render a `ProblemDetail` (`type`/`title`/`status`/`detail`) with the exception's original HTTP status. This is more specific than the catch-all `Exception` handler, so it wins over the 500 path, and it produces `application/problem+json` on the same code path the 400 handler already uses.\n\n**2. `OwnerRestControllerV1` \u2014 409/429 now carry a body**\nThe 429 (per-day limit) and two 409s (city hard-capacity, duplicate identity) previously returned `new ResponseEntity<>(HttpStatus.\u2026)` with **empty bodies**. They now `throw new ResponseStatusException(status, detail)`, so the new handler renders a proper problem body. The HTTP status codes are unchanged.\n\n**3. `PostcodeValidator` \u2014 null-safe city lookup (root cause of the failing 400 test)**\nThe new acceptance test removes `city`, which is `@NotNull`, so it should yield a 400. But the class-level `PostcodeValidator` did `CITY_REGION.get(owner.getCity())`, and `Map.of(...).get(null)` throws an NPE \u2014 Hibernate Validator wrapped it as a `ValidationException`, aborting the whole validation pass and surfacing a 500 that masked the real 400. I made the city lookup null-safe (a null city is treated as unknown-region and accepts the postcode, exactly as the validator's documented intent for unknown-region cities), letting the `@NotNull` violation flow through to `MethodArgumentNotValidException` \u2192 400.\n\n**Verification**: The provided `AcceptanceTest` passes, the full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`), and a throwaway test I ran-then-removed confirmed the 409 and 429 paths return `application/problem+json` with all four required members.",
