    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change is coherent, additive, and idiomatic for this codebase.\n\n- **Rejection body-building lives in the right unit.** The controller stops hand-rolling bare `ResponseEntity<>(HttpStatus.X)` responses and instead throws `ResponseStatusException`, letting the central `ExceptionControllerAdvice` produce the RFC7807 body. This is the correct division of labor \u2014 the controller states *why* it rejects (via the reason string), and the advice owns *how* rejections are serialized. It removes the divergence where these three paths would otherwise have to build problem details inline.\n\n- **The new handler matches its siblings exactly.** `handleResponseStatusException` mirrors `handleDataIntegrityViolationException` and `handleGeneralException`: same `logger.warn(...)` shape, same reuse of the private `detailBuild(...)` helper, same `ResponseEntity.status(status).body(problemDetail)` return. No duplication is introduced and the helper is reused rather than copied. It's slotted in among the other `@ExceptionHandler` methods naturally.\n\n- **`Objects.toString(e.getReason(), status.getReasonPhrase())` fallback** is consistent with the `Objects.toString(..., default)` idiom already used in `handleMethodArgumentNotValidException`, so a maintainer would find it familiar.\n\n- **The `PostcodeValidator` null-guard is minimal and well-placed.** It's a localized defensive tweak with a comment explaining the delegation of the null-city concern to the `@NotNull` rule, and it preserves the existing \"unknown region accepts any postcode\" branch rather than adding a parallel code path. It doesn't turn the validator into a catch-all.\n\nNothing is becoming a god-class here \u2014 the advice is exactly where cross-cutting exception-to-response mapping belongs in a Spring app, and this addition keeps it cohesive.",
